WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.340
Speaker 1: Today, I'm going to be speaking
with Spencer Head Worth, who's assistant professor

00:00:04.340 --> 00:00:11.070
of sociology at Purdue University, affiliated
scholar of the American Bar Foundation, also

00:00:11.070 --> 00:00:17.180
author of the book Policing Welfare Punitive,
Adversarial ISM in Public Assistance.

00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:18.180
It's great to have you on.

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:19.180
I really appreciate your time.

00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:20.240
Speaker 5: Thanks for having me.

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:21.289
It's good to be here.

00:00:21.289 --> 00:00:24.960
Speaker 1: So, you know, I think maybe to
get us into this conversation, oftentimes

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:32.529
in popular culture, people will point to the
Ronald Reagan stories about welfare queens

00:00:32.529 --> 00:00:38.590
as really raising the level of suspicion around
welfare programs and so-called free riders.

00:00:38.590 --> 00:00:44.400
How far back can we trace that suspicion about
people receiving benefits they don't really

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:46.200
deserve and this type of idea?

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:47.930
Speaker 5: Sure.

00:00:47.930 --> 00:00:52.610
So it goes back about as far as organized
poverty relief programs go back.

00:00:52.610 --> 00:00:58.059
So you're going to look at English poor law
in the 15th century and the fourteen hundreds

00:00:58.059 --> 00:00:59.059
in England.

00:00:59.059 --> 00:01:06.560
And in those very early versions of assistance
programs, there were provisions for inspectors

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:11.810
basically to check up on people and see if
they were drinking or gambling with the assistance

00:01:11.810 --> 00:01:14.609
that they were getting from the state.

00:01:14.609 --> 00:01:19.719
And in the United States, again, attracts
back to the kind of earliest versions of public

00:01:19.719 --> 00:01:23.779
assistance programs that were out there.

00:01:23.779 --> 00:01:31.510
And in the 20th century, when we get food
stamps, the predecessor to snap, the kind

00:01:31.510 --> 00:01:37.710
of earliest food stamp provisions included
checking on people to see if they were violating

00:01:37.710 --> 00:01:38.710
rules.

00:01:38.710 --> 00:01:42.159
And there's a long history that's intertwined.

00:01:42.159 --> 00:01:48.529
There really is is almost no historical period
where welfare programs did not include some

00:01:48.529 --> 00:01:55.130
kind of suspicion checking evaluation types
of mechanisms.

00:01:55.130 --> 00:01:59.299
That said, it certainly does ramp up in the
Reagan administration, in the United States

00:01:59.299 --> 00:02:05.249
as a politically and popularly salient issue
and a period when we get several key legal

00:02:05.249 --> 00:02:06.249
changes.

00:02:06.249 --> 00:02:16.290
Speaker 1: So if a certain program has requirements
in order to qualify, it seems intuitive that

00:02:16.290 --> 00:02:21.540
you would have some mechanism for policing
that and ensuring that the people that are

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:24.110
getting the benefits actually qualify.

00:02:24.110 --> 00:02:34.519
Now, in in some theoretical sense, if one
eliminated all of this bureaucracy for policing,

00:02:34.519 --> 00:02:37.129
there's some cost to that bureaucracy.

00:02:37.129 --> 00:02:43.930
In theory, it might actually, in the net cost
less not to worry about the so-called free

00:02:43.930 --> 00:02:47.379
riders and eliminate the cost of the bureaucracy.

00:02:47.379 --> 00:02:50.290
Has that been studied with any particular
programs?

00:02:50.290 --> 00:02:56.990
Speaker 5: So economists have looked into
this, at least to some degree, with regard

00:02:56.990 --> 00:03:04.709
to UBI type of system, right, a universal
basic income, the idea of taking a means tested

00:03:04.709 --> 00:03:12.620
program like SNAP like ten, if that we have
now, and completely eliminating the expenses

00:03:12.620 --> 00:03:17.180
associated with the policing mechanisms, it's
really hard to tell.

00:03:17.180 --> 00:03:21.080
You can do some things, some very basic, you
know, back of the napkin.

00:03:21.080 --> 00:03:22.480
How much are we recovering?

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:28.379
Poor investigation type of math, but that
does not take account for whatever deterrent

00:03:28.379 --> 00:03:35.439
effect the existence of fraud investigation
has, the kind of broader symbolic components

00:03:35.439 --> 00:03:41.389
of the system that have an influence on people's
behavior, which there is some influence.

00:03:41.389 --> 00:03:43.129
But it's really hard to determine how.

00:03:43.129 --> 00:03:48.530
It's the kind of counterfactual where we don't
have these programs, we don't have data, really.

00:03:48.530 --> 00:03:49.530
Right.

00:03:49.530 --> 00:03:51.230
So it's tough to say.

00:03:51.230 --> 00:03:56.810
And for the book I The Primary did our interviews
with people who work in fraud investigation

00:03:56.810 --> 00:04:01.329
investigators and data analysts and managers
of these units and so forth.

00:04:01.329 --> 00:04:05.870
And they, you know, perhaps unsurprisingly,
given it's their job, but they think that

00:04:05.870 --> 00:04:11.280
that deterrent effect, which is hard to measure,
is is really substantial.

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:15.810
So it's hard to say
Speaker 1: in in in policing by law enforcement.

00:04:15.810 --> 00:04:24.729
We have many examples of how a law or a statute
can just kind of be written agnostically on

00:04:24.729 --> 00:04:30.140
paper, but can be applied in discriminatory
ways or selectively.

00:04:30.140 --> 00:04:35.700
Can you talk a little bit about when it comes
to policing of welfare programs, does the

00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:36.700
same thing apply?

00:04:36.700 --> 00:04:42.310
And if so, how do we see kind of the discriminate
discriminative application of some of these

00:04:42.310 --> 00:04:44.990
programs of policing mechanisms, rather?

00:04:44.990 --> 00:04:51.590
Speaker 5: So there are ways that these programs,
the way some of the ways that these programs

00:04:51.590 --> 00:04:59.560
function, do have some implications, I think,
for inequities in who's investigated, who's

00:04:59.560 --> 00:05:03.900
potentially charged with welfare fraud, who
has these charges substantiated against them.

00:05:03.900 --> 00:05:08.400
You know, I will note that all of the or nearly
all of the people that I spoke to were adamant

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:11.930
that they do not discriminate on the basis
of race.

00:05:11.930 --> 00:05:17.270
They have no feelings about that one way or
the other.

00:05:17.270 --> 00:05:22.330
One of the basic facts here is that they mostly
investigate women because women are disproportionately

00:05:22.330 --> 00:05:23.960
participants in the program.

00:05:23.960 --> 00:05:26.520
So there's that there's that difference.

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:30.930
Speaker 1: I guess the question there would
be, are they investigated at a greater rate

00:05:30.930 --> 00:05:32.910
than their level of participation?

00:05:32.910 --> 00:05:38.539
Speaker 5: They they don't keep statistics
on the characteristics of the people that

00:05:38.539 --> 00:05:40.169
they investigate.

00:05:40.169 --> 00:05:41.300
They told me that they don't.

00:05:41.300 --> 00:05:42.949
So I can't say that for sure.

00:05:42.949 --> 00:05:49.100
I can say that, for example, being a person
with children as opposed to a person without

00:05:49.100 --> 00:05:55.319
children has at least some effect on increasing
your likelihood of becoming the subject of

00:05:55.319 --> 00:05:57.250
a serious investigation.

00:05:57.250 --> 00:06:03.650
So the one of the basic mechanisms here is
they're trying to figure out which cases are

00:06:03.650 --> 00:06:08.200
most promising so they have more potential
investigations and they can pursue.

00:06:08.200 --> 00:06:10.160
So they choose which ones to go after.

00:06:10.160 --> 00:06:11.160
Right.

00:06:11.160 --> 00:06:15.190
And one of the main things that they're looking
for is a higher dollar value.

00:06:15.190 --> 00:06:18.490
So if you're the head of a household with
children, you're probably getting a higher

00:06:18.490 --> 00:06:21.070
dollar value in benefits than if you're not.

00:06:21.070 --> 00:06:25.509
So that's an example of just kind of a mathematical
risk factor.

00:06:25.509 --> 00:06:31.550
Another thing that's going on here is the
cases that are most promising to investigate

00:06:31.550 --> 00:06:34.400
are cases with more evidentiary availability.

00:06:34.400 --> 00:06:35.400
Right.

00:06:35.400 --> 00:06:39.250
So if you can get more information about a
person, you're more likely to spend more time

00:06:39.250 --> 00:06:44.580
on that investigation to make it a priority
because you can get more access to evidence

00:06:44.580 --> 00:06:47.009
that you can use to substantiate your charges.

00:06:47.009 --> 00:06:53.240
So that means if you are, quote unquote on
paper in various ways already with government

00:06:53.240 --> 00:06:58.159
agencies, whether that be criminal justice
agencies, agencies of the welfare state, whatever

00:06:58.159 --> 00:07:04.580
it is, if you have kids in school, all of
these are potential sources of information

00:07:04.580 --> 00:07:08.900
that welfare fraud investigators can draw
upon to figure out how much money you make,

00:07:08.900 --> 00:07:11.460
who lives in your house, these kinds of things.

00:07:11.460 --> 00:07:19.500
So being more recorded by the state has this
sort of implication of also making you potentially

00:07:19.500 --> 00:07:23.930
more interesting, you know, someone to investigate
because this evidence is out there in ways

00:07:23.930 --> 00:07:26.879
it might not be for someone who's less recorded
with the state.

00:07:26.879 --> 00:07:34.630
Speaker 1: When we talk about fraud, people,
oftentimes the application processes for these

00:07:34.630 --> 00:07:39.360
programs are not particularly simple.

00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:45.030
And there is, it seems to me, at least in
intent, a big difference between fraud and

00:07:45.030 --> 00:07:48.450
errors made during the application process.

00:07:48.450 --> 00:07:55.300
Can you talk a little bit about whether mistakes
and errors or information that has since changed

00:07:55.300 --> 00:07:58.669
are considered fraud under this general umbrella?

00:07:58.669 --> 00:08:03.689
Speaker 5: So, I mean, first I would flag
the person whose research is probably the

00:08:03.689 --> 00:08:07.800
best on this is Karen Gusterson, who wrote
a book called Cheating Welfare.

00:08:07.800 --> 00:08:11.409
That's one of the inspirations for this book
that came out a few years ago with NYU Press.

00:08:11.409 --> 00:08:14.759
And, you know, she focuses on clients.

00:08:14.759 --> 00:08:20.719
And so she she does pretty deeply into this
question of intent and the construction of

00:08:20.719 --> 00:08:21.719
intent.

00:08:21.719 --> 00:08:24.259
And when is a mistake just a mistake?

00:08:24.259 --> 00:08:29.270
One thing that I can say for sure from my
evidence and comparing it with previous work

00:08:29.270 --> 00:08:35.830
like Corrine's that looks at the client side
is that the investigators are much more likely

00:08:35.830 --> 00:08:42.020
to see things as intentional and present them
as intentional than our right.

00:08:42.020 --> 00:08:48.040
And that is in part due to the structural
factors pertaining here.

00:08:48.040 --> 00:08:53.660
So they are the individual investigators and
the broader units of which they are parts

00:08:53.660 --> 00:09:00.110
are incentivized to get intentional program
violation charges, intentional fraud charges.

00:09:00.110 --> 00:09:02.970
Those are the sort of most rewarding for them
professionally.

00:09:02.970 --> 00:09:08.650
They also garner financial rewards for the
state from the federal government when you

00:09:08.650 --> 00:09:12.500
can substantiate these types of charges.

00:09:12.500 --> 00:09:20.060
So there are things that I think are borderline
that are constructed as intentional.

00:09:20.060 --> 00:09:27.820
And I mean, the broad, you know, sort of baseline
understanding here is that if you provide

00:09:27.820 --> 00:09:33.930
a piece of information to the agency and sign
your application form saying that everything

00:09:33.930 --> 00:09:39.140
that you've sworn to hear is accurate and
up to date, and then later they find evidence,

00:09:39.140 --> 00:09:43.400
whether it's from the Department of Labor
or the Department of Motor Vehicles or a privately

00:09:43.400 --> 00:09:48.330
maintained database of some kind, wherever
they get it, if they find that evidence that

00:09:48.330 --> 00:09:54.480
contradicts whatever you put on your signed
statement, that is generally considered probative

00:09:54.480 --> 00:09:57.020
proof of intentional fraud.

00:09:57.020 --> 00:10:02.380
Speaker 1: And what is the process for adjudicating
something like that?

00:10:02.380 --> 00:10:08.240
Speaker 5: So in nineteen seventy six, the
US Supreme Court ruled in Goldberg vs. Kelley

00:10:08.240 --> 00:10:14.770
that welfare benefits are a form of property
and that therefore participants in the program

00:10:14.770 --> 00:10:21.110
are entitled to some version of due process
before benefits can be terminated.

00:10:21.110 --> 00:10:26.280
So the way that that works when you're accused
of an intentional program violation is in

00:10:26.280 --> 00:10:27.280
a setting.

00:10:27.280 --> 00:10:34.060
It's called an administrative hearing, administrative
disqualification hearing perhaps that is overseen

00:10:34.060 --> 00:10:37.980
by an administrative hearing officer that
is the equivalent of a judge.

00:10:37.980 --> 00:10:43.760
And that's where the client gets an opportunity
to come in and potentially argue against whatever

00:10:43.760 --> 00:10:46.810
allegations are being made, present their
own evidence.

00:10:46.810 --> 00:10:51.241
If they have evidence that contradicts the
evidence that the welfare fraud investigator

00:10:51.241 --> 00:10:56.620
gathered and then the administrative hearing
officer makes a determination of whether the

00:10:56.620 --> 00:11:03.300
state has met their burden of proof in establishing
clear and convincing evidence of an intentional

00:11:03.300 --> 00:11:04.520
program violation.

00:11:04.520 --> 00:11:09.300
So that's one of the things that welfare fraud
investigators have to learn how to navigate

00:11:09.300 --> 00:11:15.500
and negotiate is how to work in these settings,
getting to know the particular hearing officers

00:11:15.500 --> 00:11:18.120
and what kind of standards they have.

00:11:18.120 --> 00:11:22.790
And it's, you know, one sort of advantage
that a fraud investigator has over a client

00:11:22.790 --> 00:11:27.600
is they're more familiar with the second they
potentially know the hearing officer.

00:11:27.600 --> 00:11:28.600
They've been there before.

00:11:28.600 --> 00:11:32.140
They know, you know, Miss Jones likes this
kind of evidence.

00:11:32.140 --> 00:11:34.570
Mr. Smith doesn't like this kind of evidence
right now.

00:11:34.570 --> 00:11:37.080
They're more inclined to do this or that.

00:11:37.080 --> 00:11:39.860
So that's the that's the setting where these
things are adjudicated.

00:11:39.860 --> 00:11:44.560
And one of the things that fraud investigators
learn is how to navigate and negotiate in

00:11:44.560 --> 00:11:45.560
those spaces.

00:11:45.560 --> 00:11:51.200
Speaker 1: One of the things I wanted to talk
a little bit about is just other systems in

00:11:51.200 --> 00:11:55.150
other countries that may overall be more efficient.

00:11:55.150 --> 00:11:58.540
And one could argue, you know, part of it
depends on your perspective, but one could

00:11:58.540 --> 00:12:02.160
argue that there's lots of inefficiencies
in in these systems.

00:12:02.160 --> 00:12:05.110
One could argue that the means testing itself
is an inefficiency.

00:12:05.110 --> 00:12:07.940
One could argue that the methods for policing
are inefficient.

00:12:07.940 --> 00:12:10.470
There's a lot of different approaches to that.

00:12:10.470 --> 00:12:17.610
But in terms of benefits and cost efficiency
overall, what should we be aspiring to?

00:12:17.610 --> 00:12:20.790
What sort of examples in other countries are
particularly well-run?

00:12:20.790 --> 00:12:26.760
Speaker 5: So I'm not an expert on international
welfare states, but I will say that there

00:12:26.760 --> 00:12:31.820
is, I think, a very strong case to be made
for a universal basic income type of system

00:12:31.820 --> 00:12:38.910
where you remove all of this administrative
overhead, all this bureaucratic red tape,

00:12:38.910 --> 00:12:44.500
and create a much smoother flowing system
without all of these hurdles and barriers

00:12:44.500 --> 00:12:47.710
and all of the associated costs and expenses.

00:12:47.710 --> 00:12:53.780
And so people who are experts in this area,
at least to the extent that I've read them,

00:12:53.780 --> 00:13:02.740
you know, generally associate a system like
ours that focuses on means testing with the

00:13:02.740 --> 00:13:10.530
almost inherent or inescapable circumstance
of rule enforcement being a big part of it.

00:13:10.530 --> 00:13:11.530
Right.

00:13:11.530 --> 00:13:16.720
And this process of determining who's in and
who's out being a costly endeavor.

00:13:16.720 --> 00:13:22.270
So in a system where you can eliminate those
administrative expenses, you're saving that

00:13:22.270 --> 00:13:23.270
money.

00:13:23.270 --> 00:13:29.220
So I think that there's a definitely a good
case to be made for more of a universal system.

00:13:29.220 --> 00:13:33.750
I mean, something like we have now in the
United States, but just based on age for Social

00:13:33.750 --> 00:13:39.330
Security benefits and Medicare benefits in
Speaker 1: such a system, what both when it

00:13:39.330 --> 00:13:46.920
comes to UBI and so-called free college, there's
the criticism that you're also giving stuff

00:13:46.920 --> 00:13:48.420
to the very, very rich.

00:13:48.420 --> 00:13:53.380
Now, the very, very rich, statistically are
a small percentage of the economy.

00:13:53.380 --> 00:13:56.730
But by sort of definition, is that something
we should worry about or not?

00:13:56.730 --> 00:14:01.600
Speaker 5: But I mean, I won't tell people
what to worry about or what to not worry about,

00:14:01.600 --> 00:14:06.380
but there I mean, there is an inherent logic
to a means tested system.

00:14:06.380 --> 00:14:09.600
It makes a lot of sense, I think, in an abstract
world.

00:14:09.600 --> 00:14:10.600
Yeah.

00:14:10.600 --> 00:14:14.990
Or just, you know, on paper that people in
need should be the people that receive things.

00:14:14.990 --> 00:14:18.700
But it just gets very complicated when you
come down to the process.

00:14:18.700 --> 00:14:24.290
OK, what is neat, how are we going to calculate
that that's contentious in and of itself.

00:14:24.290 --> 00:14:26.460
And then you have these these administrative
costs.

00:14:26.460 --> 00:14:29.690
I think they're often not visible publicly.

00:14:29.690 --> 00:14:30.690
Right.

00:14:30.690 --> 00:14:33.390
Or not really prominent in the popular imagination.

00:14:33.390 --> 00:14:39.560
And again, there is this inherent logic to
targeting aid to needy people.

00:14:39.560 --> 00:14:44.560
But where the rubber hits the road, it's a
lot more complicated than just see who's needy,

00:14:44.560 --> 00:14:45.560
give it to them.

00:14:45.560 --> 00:14:46.940
Don't give it to someone who's not.

00:14:46.940 --> 00:14:49.060
Speaker 1: Yeah, yeah, that's for sure.

00:14:49.060 --> 00:14:50.150
That's for sure.

00:14:50.150 --> 00:14:55.540
The book is Policing Welfare, Punitive, Adversarial
ISM and Public Assistance.

00:14:55.540 --> 00:15:00.080
We've been speaking with the book's author,
Spencer Headwork, who's also assistant professor

00:15:00.080 --> 00:15:02.910
of sociology at Purdue University.

00:15:02.910 --> 00:15:05.210
So great having you on today and really appreciate
your time.

00:15:05.210 --> 00:15:06.210
Speaker 5: Thanks, David.

00:15:06.210 --> 00:15:06.590
It was a pleasure.

